Let $\Sigma$ be a finite alphabet and $\Sigma^*$ the set of finite words over $\Sigma$. For a word $w \in \Sigma^*$, define the prefix-suffix square completion relation $\Rightarrow$ on $\Sigma^*$ as follows:
- (suffix square completion) $w \Rightarrow wx$ whenever $w$ has a suffix of the form $yx$ with $|x| \ge 1$ (so that $wx$ ends with the square $yxyx$ — i.e., $w = uyx$ and the new word $wx = uyxyx$);
- (prefix square completion) $w \Rightarrow xw$ whenever $w$ has a prefix of the form $xy$ with $|x| \ge 1$ (so that $xw$ starts with the square $xyxy$).

Let $\Rightarrow^*$ be the reflexive-transitive closure of $\Rightarrow$. For a language $L \subseteq \Sigma^*$, define
$$\mathrm{PSC}^*(L) = \{ v \in \Sigma^* : \exists\, w \in L,\ w \Rightarrow^* v \}.$$

Carry out a systematic study of the languages $\mathrm{PSC}^*(L)$ obtained by iterated prefix-suffix square completion when the initial set $L$ ranges over distinguished families of languages, in particular:
(i) singleton sets $L = \{w\}$;
(ii) arbitrary finite sets;
(iii) regular languages.

Investigate structural properties such as membership of $\mathrm{PSC}^*(L)$ in the Chomsky hierarchy (regular, context-free, context-sensitive), closure properties, decidability of membership, growth, and the structure of the resulting languages in each case.

